"Building a Telescope | VirtualDemos",
"Building a Telescope",
"<strong>Setups in the demo:</strong> Magnifier; Compound microscope; Keplerian telescope; Galilean telescope; Terrestrial telescope; Start from scratch.",
"The rail is drawn to scale from the side, in centimeters. Light from the object (here the Moon, far away) passes through the lenses into the eye, and every ray is traced. Two small pictures show what the eye sees through the instrument and what it would see without it, at the same scale.",
"Click a lens to change its focal length, its diameter or its place on the rail; click the eye to change the size of its pupil or its place. “Put it at the exit pupil” moves the eye to the spot where all the light leaving the eyepiece passes through.",
"The readouts give the angular magnification M (how many times bigger things look; a minus sign means the view is upside down), the exit pupil (the width of the beam that leaves the eyepiece), the apparent field of view, the image brightness and <strong>Light from a star</strong>: how many times more light from a star reaches the eye through the telescope than reaches the bare eye.",
"The lenses are ideal thin lenses, with one color of light and no blur from diffraction (its limit is quoted under Light and field). Real lenses split colors, as the next worksheet shows.",
"Record reading stores the focal lengths, the objective’s diameter and every readout. Export to worksheet sends the readings to the Data table below; Clear starts a new series, which goes into a new copy of the table.",
"Build a simple telescope from two lenses and find out what sets its magnification and what sets how much light it gathers.",
"<strong>Two lenses.</strong> A refracting telescope has a large lens at the front, the <strong>objective</strong>, and a small lens near the eye, the <strong>eyepiece</strong>. The objective makes a small image of a distant object at its focal point, a distance \\(f_1\\) (its focal length) behind it. The eyepiece, placed so that its own focal point falls on that image, works as a magnifying glass. The two lenses are then \\(f_1 + f_2\\) apart and the light leaves the eyepiece parallel, so a relaxed eye sees the image sharply.",
"<strong>Magnification.</strong> The telescope makes things look bigger by the ratio of the focal lengths: magnification = \\(f_1 / f_2\\). A long objective and a short eyepiece give a high magnification. The image is upside down, which does not matter in astronomy.",
"<strong>Light gathering.</strong> A star is so far away that it looks like a point, even through a telescope. What a telescope does for a star is collect more of its light. The light collected depends on the area of the objective, and area goes as the square of the diameter \\(D\\): a lens twice as wide collects four times as much light. Your eye at night collects light through its pupil, about {#} mm ({#} cm) across when it has adjusted to the dark.",
"A telescope has an objective of focal length {#} cm and an eyepiece of focal length {#} cm. What is its magnification? How far apart should the two lenses be?",
"If you swap the {#} cm eyepiece for a {#} cm one, what happens to the magnification?",
"Telescope A has an objective {#} cm across and telescope B one {#} cm across. Which collects more light from a star, and how many times more?",
"Never look at the Sun through a telescope, binoculars or a camera without a proper solar filter: the concentrated sunlight can blind you instantly. No hazards in the virtual lab.",
"<strong>Part {#}. Magnification.</strong> Choose the Keplerian telescope, which looks at the full Moon. Set the eye’s pupil to {#} mm, its size at night. Record a reading. Then change the eyepiece to a focal length of {#} cm, slide it so it is \\(f_1 + f_2\\) = {#} cm behind the objective (the final image is then at infinity), put the eye at the exit pupil and record a reading. Do the same with a {#} cm eyepiece, {#} cm behind the objective. Send the readings to the worksheet, then clear them.",
"<strong>Part {#}. Light gathering.</strong> Choose the Keplerian telescope again and check that the eye’s pupil is still {#} mm. Keeping the {#} cm eyepiece, record a reading with objectives {#}, {#}, {#}, {#}, {#} and {#} cm across. Send the readings to the worksheet.",
